    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/Campus-Police-Officer_R0003508": {
      "title": "Campus Police Officer - Entry Level OK",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Campus Police Officer - Entry Level OK LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Campus Police - Midnight Shift DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Worcester Polytechnic Institute is an R1 research university in Worcester, MA and has an opening for Campus Police Officer. This role is open to both experienced officers and candidates looking to start a career in public safety and law enforcement. Entry-level candidates are encouraged to apply. WPI will sponsor selected candidates through the full Massachusetts police academy. If you're already POST-certified, you'll bring your experience to a college campus with strong community engagement and a supportive department. In this role, you'll help keep our students, faculty, staff, and visitors safe. ** This position is assigned to the third shift (overnight/midnight hours). ** JOB DESCRIPTION Protect life and property on the WPI campus and the surrounding area. Respond to emergency calls including crimes in progress, medical emergencies, fire and intrusion alarms, and accidents. Investigate, document, and write reports. Patrol campus buildings and grounds on foot and in vehicles. Provide everyday public safety services like escorts and lock-out assists, treating every member of the community with respect. Promote crime prevention and safety awareness across campus. Enforce Massachusetts state law and WPI safety, security, and traffic regulations. Take proper police action at scenes, administer first aid, gather and preserve evidence, interview witnesses and suspects, and testify in court when needed. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Must be at least 21 years of age at time of hire to meet MA requirements for the License to Carry and police academy admission High school diploma or equivalent. Valid Massachusetts driver's license. Dependable, honest, and able to communicate clearly with a wide range of people. Able to stay calm under pressure, think quickly, and use good judgment. Physically fit and able to complete the full Massachusetts police academy if not already certified. Willing to obtain a Massachusetts firearm license and pass firearms qualification using the weapon issued by the WPI Police Department, plus any required in-service training. Certified candidates must hold an active POST certification and have a clean disciplinary record. ** This position is assigned to the third shift (overnight/midnight hours). ** WPI will sponsor selected entry-level candidates through the full Massachusetts police academy (MPTC basic recruit). Candidates who are already POST-certified can join the department directly. All candidates must pass a thorough background investigation and CORI check. Upon preliminary offer, candidates must complete and pass a full medical exam, drug testing, and psychological exam. Compensation: $28.39–$34.65 per hour, depending on experience and qualifications, in accordance with the collective bargaining agreement. Overtime opportunities are available as outlined in the agreement. FLSA STATUS United States of America (Non-Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/Program-Coordinator_R0003733": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Program Coordinator LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Robotics Engineering DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Worcester Polytechnic Institute seeks a full-time Program Coordinator for a six-year, NSF-funded S-STEM scholarship program that will support 70 annual scholarships for low-income undergraduate and master's students across four degree programs (RBE, MME, CEAE, IMGD). The program combines scholarships with an integrated set of academic and professional development activities: VINE (Vertically Integrated Nexus Experience) research projects that connect scholars across academic levels, CoHORT cross-disciplinary project teams organized around convergent research themes, a personalized Bridge curriculum for transfer and entering scholars, and a structured internship and career pathway with industry partners. The Program Coordinator is central to the daily operation and cohort continuity of the program. Working closely with the PI, a Co-PI team spanning four academic programs, an external evaluator, community college partners, and industry partners, the Coordinator serves as the program's operational hub and the primary day-to-day point of contact for scholars. Because scholars enter in overlapping annual cohorts, the position requires someone who can hold a complex, multi-year picture of every participant while running the recurring annual cycle of recruitment, selection, programming, and reporting. JOB DESCRIPTION Essential Responsibilities: Scholar Recruitment, Selection, and Onboarding Manage the annual application and selection cycle end to end, including the unified application process, the Spring deadline for fall cohort entry, and the mid-year replacement cycle that maintains full cohort strength. Work with departmental advisors and Faculty Champions each spring to identify high-potential sophomores eligible for junior-year entry; coordinate with the Office of Student Aid & Financial Literacy to confirm financial eligibility. Support Faculty Champion follow-up with admitted students prior to enrollment. Support Co-PI recruiting visits to community college partners (QCC, MWCC, MassBay, NECC, Manchester CC, Berkshire CC, Holyoke CC) and to the University of Puerto Rico–Mayagüez, including scheduling, materials, and follow-up with prospective transfer scholars over the 12–18 month pre-transfer window. Serve as a member of the selection committee alongside the PI and Co-PIs; administer the holistic review rubric, coordinate faculty recommendation letters, and schedule finalist interviews. Coordinate onboarding for each incoming cohort, including orientation, Bridge placement, faculty champion assignment, and scholarship award notification. Cohort Management and Scholar Tracking Build and maintain the centralized scholar database tracking all participants, with semester-by-semester records of GPA, program participation, financial aid status, and milestone completion. Monitor early warning indicators (academic, financial, and engagement) and escalate concerns promptly to the PI and the scholar's Faculty Champion so that support can be put in place before a scholar is at risk of leaving. Track scholarship eligibility, renewal decisions, and award continuation in coordination with the Office of Student Aid & Financial Literacy and the sponsored programs office. Maintain accurate rosters and status records for scholars moving between stages (junior/senior undergraduate, accelerated BS/MS, master's) and manage the replacement cycle when a scholar exits. Mentoring and Program Activity Coordination Coordinate mentor–mentee matching across the program, pairing scholars with near-peer mentors and Faculty Champions based on research theme, discipline, and academic level; monitor Faculty Champion caseloads against the five-scholar cap. Coordinate scholar placement into VINE vertical projects and CoHORT cross-disciplinary teams, working with faculty to align scholar interests with active projects. Support delivery of the Bridge curriculum, including coordination of the self-paced module library, the structured summer course pathway for incoming transfer students, and personalized academic plan meetings between scholars, Faculty Champions, and peer mentors. Plan and execute program events: cohort kickoffs, seminars, workshops, team showcases, industry engagement events, and the annual program meeting. Handle logistics, scheduling, catering, space, and communications. Coordinate the Scholar Advisory Council and support its members in exercising a genuine student voice in program decisions. Industry Partnerships and Career Pathways Serve as the program's liaison with industry partners on internship placements, matching scholars to internal and external opportunities. Track internship participation and job placement outcomes against program targets. Coordinate the annual Industry Advisory Board meeting, including scheduling, agenda preparation, materials, and follow-up on internship commitments. Partner with WPI Career Development Center staff to connect scholars with career services, résumé and interview preparation, and employer events. Evaluation, Reporting, and Program Administration Coordinate data collection for the external evaluator, including survey administration, focus group and interview scheduling, consent tracking, and secure transmission of scholar records in accordance with IRB protocols. Prepare data, tables, and narrative sections for NSF annual and final reports in collaboration with the PI. Schedule and staff quarterly PI team meetings; prepare agendas, circulate scholar progress and assessment summaries, and track action items to completion. Monitor programmatic (non-scholarship) budget lines in coordination with the PI and departmental administration; process travel, event, and participant support expenses. Maintain program communications: website, scholar-facing newsletters and announcements, recruitment materials, and partner-facing updates. Support dissemination activities, including conference submissions, the practitioner guide, and S-STEM community engagement. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and three or more years of relevant experience in program coordination, student support, academic administration, or grant-funded project management. An equivalent combination of education and experience will be considered. Demonstrated ability to independently manage a recurring program cycle with multiple concurrent deadlines and many participants. Strong data management skills, including experience building and maintaining participant databases, tracking longitudinal records, and producing accurate reports from them. Excellent written and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with students, faculty across disciplines, external partners, and administrative offices. Demonstrated capacity to work with students with discretion, warmth, and sound judgment. Ability to handle confidential academic and financial records appropriately and in compliance with relevant protocols. Proficiency with standard productivity and collaboration tools (spreadsheets, databases, survey platforms, shared document systems). Preferred Qualifications: Higher education administration, STEM education, engineering, or a related field. Experience coordinating a federally funded education or training program (NSF S-STEM, NRT, REU, RET, LSAMP, or similar), including familiarity with sponsor reporting requirements. Familiarity with WPI's project-based curriculum (IQP/MQP), accelerated BS/MS pathway, and transfer student infrastructure. Experience with mentor–mentee matching, cohort programming, or learning community models. Experience working with community college partners, articulation agreements, or transfer student pathways. Familiarity with program evaluation practices, IRB processes, and educational research data collection. Experience coordinating industry relationships or internship placements. Interest in or exposure to robotics, infrastructure, manufacturing, or interactive media. Attributes for Success The strongest candidate for this role is well-organized to run a six-year program on a calendar and relational for scholars to come to them first when something goes wrong. The position rewards someone who notices the quiet student, who keeps a comprehensive database of complex information, and who can move between a spreadsheet of GPA records, a conversation with an industry partner, and a scholar's office-hours drop-in without losing the thread. Work Environment Primarily an on-campus office environment. Occasional evening and weekend hours are required around program events, cohort kickoffs, and the annual meeting. Periodic travel to regional community college partners in Massachusetts and New Hampshire is expected; occasional travel to national conferences may be required. Application: Applicants should submit a cover letter and résumé or CV. The cover letter should describe relevant experience coordinating programs or supporting students, and any experience with grant-funded education programs. Compensation: $58,500 per year based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity. This position is contingent upon the availability of continue external funding. WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/Assistant-Teaching-Professor_R0003666": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Teaching Professor LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Civil, Environmental, & Architectural Engineering DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Department of Civil, Environmental, and Architectural Engineering (CEAE) at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) invites applications for a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor in intelligent construction and infrastructure systems with a broad focus on sensing, automation, and computational technologies that are reshaping how civil infrastructure is built, monitored, managed. The scope of interest spans the full asset lifecycle: robotics and automation on the construction site, uncrewed aerial systems and reality capture, instrumented and self-sensing structures, digital twins, and data-driven approaches to inspection, condition assessment, and asset management. Construction faces persistent labor shortages and schedule/cost pressure, while existing infrastructure stock ages and decarbonization pressures become more urgent. WPI seeks an educator who can prepare civil, architectural, and construction engineering students to work fluently with the technologies now being deployed against those problems and who will build a distinctive, hands-on curriculum and laboratory experience around them. This is a career-track teaching appointment with a clear path for promotion to Associate Teaching Professor and Teaching Professor. Teaching faculty at WPI are full voting members of the faculty and play a central role in curricular leadership. JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in areas such as construction automation and robotics; UAS and reality capture; sensing, instrumentation, and structural health monitoring; digital twins and BIM-integrated workflows; applied machine learning and computer vision for the built environment; and data-driven infrastructure asset management, scheduling, cost, productivity, and safety analytics. Develop new courses and laboratory modules that give students direct experience with robotic platforms, drones, sensing hardware, field data collection, and modern computational tools. Advise and mentor students in WPI’s project-based curriculum, including Major Qualifying Projects (MQP), Interactive Qualifying Projects (IQP), and graduate capstone work. Contribute to the design and stewardship of laboratory, maker, and field-testing facilities supporting instrumented and automated construction and infrastructure work. Build and maintain relationships with contractors, owners, infrastructure agencies, and technology firms so that instruction reflects current and emerging practice. Participate in departmental service, student recruiting and retention, curriculum assessment and ABET accreditation activities, and outreach. Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate by the appointment start date in civil, architectural, construction, mechanical, or robotics engineering; computer science; or a closely related field. Demonstrated technical depth in at least two of the following , with the ability to teach in both: construction robotics and automation; UAS/drone operations and reality capture; sensing, instrumentation, and structural health monitoring; digital twins and BIM-integrated workflows; applied machine learning or computer vision for the built environment; data-driven infrastructure asset management and condition assessment. Clear anchoring in civil, architectural, or construction engineering practice . The successful candidate will be able to connect computational and robotic methods to real construction operations, structural behavior, or infrastructure management, not to treat the built environment solely as an application domain. Evidence of, or clear potential for, excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching. Commitment to hands-on, project-based, and experiential instruction. Preferred Qualifications: Prior university-level teaching experience, including course and laboratory development. Professional experience in the construction industry, with an infrastructure owner or agency, or in a construction technology firm. Experience deploying robotic, sensing, or autonomous systems in field conditions Working fluency with relevant tooling: Python and modern ML frameworks, ROS, photogrammetry and LiDAR processing, sensor networks and data acquisition, BIM and computational design platforms, or construction and asset management software. Experience building industry partnerships, sponsored student projects, or continuing education and workforce development programs. A record of mentoring students from a range of backgrounds and preparing them to work on diverse teams. About the Department and WPI WPI is a global leader in project-based learning. Its distinctive undergraduate curriculum requires every student to complete substantial team-based projects addressing real problems for real stakeholders, often at one of WPI’s project centers around the world. Courses are delivered in an intensive seven-week term structure that supports focused, immersive instruction. CEAE offers ABET-accredited undergraduate programs and graduate degrees spanning civil engineering, construction project management, environmental engineering, and architectural engineering. The department is investing in the technologies that will define the next generation of infrastructure practice, and this position is central to that direction. The successful candidate will also find natural collaborators across WPI in robotics engineering, data science, electrical and computer engineering, and computer science. Worcester, Massachusetts is New England’s second-largest city, with a growing innovation economy, a substantial construction and real estate development sector, and easy access to Boston, Providence, and Hartford. How to Apply Applications should be submitted through WPI’s online application system and include: A cover letter describing the candidate’s interest in and fit for the position. A curriculum vitae. A statement of teaching philosophy and interests (2 pages maximum), including courses the candidate would be prepared to teach and one or two new courses or laboratory experiences they would propose to develop. Names and contact information for three professional references. References will not be contacted without the candidate’s permission. Review of applications will begin Jan 1, 2027, and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to Prof. Nima Rahbar at nrahbar@wpi.edu  . Compensation Salary: $80,000 - $85,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/Director--Systems-Engineering_R0003675": {
      "title": "Director, Systems Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Director, Systems Engineering LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Infrastructure Services and Architecture DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Reporting to the Assistant Chief Technology Officer, Infrastructure Services and Architecture, the Director of Systems Engineering will oversee the operation, administration, and engineering of the university's systems environment, including servers, storage, cloud computing, and virtualization, along with infrastructure DevOps, application system and security support, and Campus Safety Services. Information technology plays a vital and ever-expanding role in the institutional mission. The institution's information technology environment is highly distributed and diverse, and the Director of Systems Engineering must be an adept and informed leader who can offer strategic vision, comprehensive planning, and direct technical involvement alongside the engineers who report to the role. This senior leadership role is responsible for ensuring the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the systems that support the university's educational and operational objectives, and for the reliability of the physical access control, video surveillance, emergency radio, and emergency phone systems that support campus safety. JOB DESCRIPTION Strategic Leadership and Team Development Develop and implement a comprehensive systems strategy aligned with the university's goals and objectives. Provide strategic direction for systems operations and administration, storage, cloud computing and virtualization, application system and security support, and Campus Safety Services. Collaborate with the Assistant CTO and other senior leaders to prioritize systems initiatives and align them with institutional priorities. Develop staff, set clear performance expectations, and manage workload and operational tasks across the systems team. Work with other groups within IT and other business units as needed to develop solutions for unique system requirements, including technical and regulatory requirements. Systems, Storage, Virtualization, and Cloud Engineering Working directly with the Systems Engineers who report to this role, direct the design, implementation, and management of the university's physical and virtual server infrastructure, enterprise storage and backup platforms, and AWS and Azure cloud environments. Participate actively in solution design, technical review, and implementation planning alongside the engineering team. Ensure efficient utilization of resources through effective virtualization, capacity planning, and lifecycle management. Lead the evaluation, selection, and adoption of new platform technologies to enhance systems capabilities. Ensure new implementations are documented, supportable, and aligned with institutional standards and architecture. Systems Operations and Administration Oversee the team that performs the day-to-day operation and administration of the university's systems environment, ensuring the stability, availability, and performance of all system components. Ensure all key services, systems, and components are under appropriate monitoring and alerting. Ensure operating system patching, firmware maintenance, and lifecycle activities are performed on a defined and predictable cadence. Ensure that all documentation and databases are up to date and accurate, including asset inventory and configuration records. Serve as the senior escalation point for the team during major incidents, directing the response, restoring service, and driving root cause analysis and preventive action to completion. Ensure all systems incidents and service requests are fully resolved within the applicable SLA and maintain open lines of communication with other ITS teams, including the Service Desk. DevOps, Automation, and Process Improvement Manage the DevOps resource supporting the infrastructure organization, receiving strategic DevOps and automation requirements from the Assistant CTO and directing the work accordingly. Identify commonly performed actions, repeated requests, and recurring requirements across every area reporting to this role, including systems operations, storage, virtualization, cloud, application system support, and Campus Safety Services. Define, document, and standardize the processes behind that work so it can be measured and performed consistently by both full-time employees and the managed service provider. Identify and prioritize the automation opportunities with the greatest return on team efficiency, and direct their design, build, and adoption. Measure the effect of automation and process improvement on toil, cycle time, and error rates, and adjust priorities based on results. Promote the adoption of automation and consistent operating practice across the teams reporting to this role. Application System and Security Support Own the systems and middleware layer beneath the university's enterprise and departmental applications, including database platforms, web services, and load balancing. Ensure that these platforms are appropriately sized, patched, monitored, hardened, and recoverable. Partner with application owners and the Office of Information Security to remediate vulnerabilities and apply security controls at the system and middleware layer. Coordinate maintenance windows and upgrade planning with application stakeholders to minimize disruption to academic and administrative operations. Campus Safety Services Oversee the technology, operation, and support of campus safety systems, including physical card access, video surveillance, emergency radios, and the emergency phone system. Ensure the security, reliability, and availability of these physical security and life-safety systems. Oversee deployment, upgrade, and lifecycle planning for campus safety technology, including coverage associated with building renovation and construction projects. Collaborate with Campus Police, Facilities, and external vendors and integrators to align technology capability with campus safety requirements. Security, Compliance, and Resilience Ensure all systems components meet institutional security and compliance requirements, and implement best practices for securing server, storage, virtualization, cloud, and campus safety infrastructure. Ensure system designs and procedures are aligned with, and capable of enforcing, university policies and best practices. Ensure backup, recovery, and business continuity capabilities are designed, documented, and regularly tested against defined recovery objectives. Ensure vulnerabilities are remediated within institutional service level targets and provide the Information Security operations team with the access and tooling required to identify, locate, and isolate systems involved in security incidents. Support audit activity and the production of control evidence, and assist Information Security in drafting policies that overlap with the systems team. Budget, Vendor, and Resource Management Develop and manage the operating and capital budget for the systems team and Campus Safety Services technology. Ensure efficient use of resources and identify opportunities for cost savings and license optimization. Oversee vendor relationships and negotiate contracts for systems, storage, cloud, and campus safety related services and products, interacting directly with vendors, resellers, and integrators. Plan and forecast refresh and renewal cycles to support multi-year budget development. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: 4-year bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Engineering, Information Systems, or Information Technology, or equivalent experience. A bachelor's degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, Engineering, Information Systems, or a related field is preferred. Minimum of 7 years of experience, including direct leadership of a systems, infrastructure, or operations team. Strong leadership and team management skills, with the ability to develop and retain technical staff. Excellent strategic thinking and planning abilities. Deep understanding of server, storage, virtualization, and cloud computing technologies across both Windows and Linux environments. Working knowledge of the middleware layer supporting enterprise applications, including database platforms, web services, and load balancing. Ability to direct DevOps and automation resources against strategic requirements set by the Assistant CTO. Knowledge of physical access control, video surveillance, and emergency communications systems. Strong problem-solving and decision-making capabilities, including the ability to serve as a senior escalation point during major incidents. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Experience developing and managing operating and capital budgets and negotiating with vendors. Preferred Certifications: Certifications are preferred, not required, and no candidate is expected to hold all of them. Credentials relevant to WPI's technology stack include: Windows Server: Microsoft Certified: Windows Server Administrator Associate (Exam AZ-802), or the predecessor Windows Server Hybrid Administrator Associate (Exams AZ-800 and AZ-801). Cloud platforms: Microsoft Certified: Azure Administrator Associate (AZ-104) or Azure Solutions Architect Expert (AZ-305); AWS Certified SysOps Administrator - Associate or AWS Certified Solutions Architect. Red Hat Enterprise Linux: Red Hat Certified System Administrator (RHCSA, EX200) or Red Hat Certified Engineer (RHCE); the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Deployment and Systems Management (EX403) additionally covers Red Hat Satellite. Nutanix: Nutanix Certified Professional - Multicloud Infrastructure (NCP-MCI), or Nutanix Certified Master - Multicloud Infrastructure (NCM-MCI). NetApp: NetApp Certified Data Administrator, ONTAP (NCDA), or a NetApp Certified Implementation Engineer (NCIE) specialty such as Data Protection or SAN. Rubrik: Rubrik Certified System Administrator (RCSA). Salary Grade 11: $109,000 – $135,000 per year (depending on experience) Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.  Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/Post-Doctoral-Fellow_R0003670": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Post-Doctoral Fellow LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Biomedical Engineering - NFR JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Artificial Intelligence and Modeling Laboratory for Cardiovascular Diseases (AIMCardio Lab) at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) is seeking applications for two postdoc research positions. We are looking for highly motivated, collaborative, and enthusiastic researchers with expertise in fluid-structure interaction, computational and experimental fluid mechanics, and/or scientific machine learning, with applications to cardiovascular diseases and medical devices. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on performance and funding availability. Successful candidates are expected to commit to a minimum two-year appointment. JOB DESCRIPTION AIMCardio Lab   The lab conducts cutting-edge research in computational and experimental fluid-structure interaction, cardiovascular biomechanics, digital health, and AI-driven modeling. The laboratory maintains long-standing collaborations with leading hospitals and medical centers across the United States, including the Hospital for Sick Children (Toronto), Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia (Philadelphia), and Columbia University Irving Medical Center (New York). We also collaborate closely with major medical device companies, including Abbott, Edwards Lifesciences, Boston Scientific, and Medtronic, to develop next-generation cardiovascular technologies and translational solutions. More information about our research can be found at:  AIMCardioLab.wpi.ed u   Required Qualifications   PhD. in Mechanical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Applied Mathematics, Computer Science, or a closely related field.   Demonstrated research excellence through publications for postdoc applicants in one of the following areas:   Experimental fluid mechanics and advanced flow visualization techniques (e.g., Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV), flow imaging, or related experimental methods).   Fluid-structure interaction (FSI), computational fluid dynamics (CFD), finite element analysis (FEA), or multiphysics simulations, with strong programming and computational skills.   Scientific machine learning , including physics-informed neural networks, operator-learning methods, surrogate modeling, and data-driven modeling of physical systems.     Preferred Qualifications   Experience conducting research in physiological fluid mechanics and cardiovascular flow systems.   Experience contributing to competitive research grant proposals   Experience in innovation-driven technology development, intellectual property generation, or commercialization activities (e.g., invention disclosures or patent applications).   Strong mentoring skills and a demonstrated ability to supervise graduate students and undergraduate researchers .   Excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary research teams.     What We Offer   Opportunities to work on high-impact projects at the intersection of cardiovascular biomechanics, artificial intelligence, and translational medicine.   Access to state-of-the-art computational and experimental research facilities.   Extensive collaborations with leading clinicians, researchers, and medical device companies.   A highly interdisciplinary and supportive research environment with strong opportunities for career development in academia, industry, and entrepreneurship.       Application Instructions :   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis and will remain open until all positions are filled. Please submit the following materials as a single PDF file:   A brief cover letter outlining your expertise , research background, and research interests   A current curriculum vitae (CV)   Academic transcripts for all completed degrees (Bachelor’s, Master’s, and Ph.D., if applicable)   Full PDF copies of 3-5 representative peer-reviewed journal publications   Contact information for three professional references   Compensation: Salary: $50,000 - $60,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/Digital-Marketing-Specialist_R0003692": {
      "title": "Digital Marketing Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Digital Marketing Specialist LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Marketing Technology & Metrics DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Digital Marketing Specialist supports the execution, reporting, and optimization of paid digital marketing campaigns across WPI's undergraduate recruitment funnel. This is an entry-level, data-forward role focused on the day-to-day operation of campaigns, building and launching ads, maintaining accurate tracking, and turning campaign data into clear, actionable reporting. The ideal candidate has a data and analytics background and a strong interest in marketing operations, with a desire to grow into a well-rounded digital marketer. The Specialist reports to the Director of Digital Marketing & Performance and works closely with the Senior Digital Marketing Specialist and the broader Marketing Operations team, who provide day-to-day guidance, mentorship, and technical support. JOB DESCRIPTION Execute paid digital advertising campaigns for undergraduate recruitment (45%): Build, launch, and manage paid campaigns across platforms including Google Ads, Meta (Facebook/Instagram), YouTube, Reddit, and other channels as assigned Execute full campaign setup: audiences, targeting, creative placement, tracking URLs, and pre-launch quality assurance/testing Implement conversion tracking and UTM parameters using the team's pre-agreed tracking structure Coordinate with the Senior Digital Marketing Specialist and Marketing Operations team to align execution with campaign strategy and timelines Manage day-to-day campaign operations across the undergraduate portfolio, monitoring delivery, budget pacing, and flighting to keep campaigns on schedule and on budget Build ads by adapting content across formats (search, paid social, video, and display) and platforms, confirming specs, tracking, and landing pages are correct before launch Build and maintain audience segments and targeting, including keyword lists, custom and lookalike audiences, retargeting pools, and exclusions Set up and manage conversion tracking, pixels, and tags in coordination with the analytics and operations workflow (e.g., Google Tag Manager) Partner with the Growth Marketing and Creative teams to translate campaign strategy and creative briefs into built, launch-ready campaigns Maintain campaign calendars, launch checklists, and account hygiene (naming conventions, structure, and budgets) across platforms Testing, analysis, and reporting automation (20%): Design, set up, and manage a testing program across undergraduate campaigns, including A/B and multivariate tests on creative, audiences, bids, and landing pages Plan and coordinate brand lift and messaging studies (e.g., YouTube and Meta brand lift, message and concept testing) to measure awareness and message effectiveness, and report on results Analyze test and campaign data from Google Analytics 4 (GA4), ad platforms, and other sources to surface insights, validate hypotheses, and inform optimization decisions Build, maintain, and continuously improve performance reports and dashboards across all ad platforms; monitor pacing, spend, and key performance indicators for undergraduate campaigns Collaborate with the Director of Digital Marketing & Performance, Senior Digital Marketing Specialist, and Marketing Operations team to automate reporting across platforms, developing and refining the tools and workflows that consolidate reporting and reduce manual effort over time Support attribution and funnel measurement efforts by ensuring clean, consistent, well-structured data capture across campaigns Campaign optimization (20%): Execute agreed-upon optimizations across undergraduate campaigns, adjusting targeting, budgets, bids, creative, and landing page routing to improve conversion performance Apply test learnings and performance data to optimize live campaigns, adjusting bids, budgets, and creative rotation accordingly Reallocate spend toward higher-performing campaigns and platforms within established guidelines to maximize efficiency against budget Troubleshoot tracking, delivery, and performance issues with support from the Director of Digital Marketing & Performance Marketing operations support (15%): Maintain campaign documentation, naming conventions, tracking libraries, and process records for the Marketing Operations team Support tag management, pixel implementation, and QA of forms, landing pages, and tracking pre- and post-launch Assist with digital marketing requests from campus partners (tracking URLs, QR codes, analytics pulls) and contribute to process automation and efficiency initiatives Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree required 0 to 2 years of relevant experience; internships, co-ops, and academic project work count toward this range Exposure to data analysis, reporting, or analytics tools in an academic or professional setting Experience collaborating within a team environment preferred Strong analytical and data literacy skills, with an aptitude for turning data into clear, actionable recommendations Familiarity with AI tools and their application to marketing operations and analytics, for example using AI to automate and streamline reporting, assist with data analysis and insight generation, and support campaign and content workflows Proficiency (or a strong willingness to develop it) in applying AI-enabled tools to everyday work to improve efficiency, accuracy, and speed across reporting, analytics, and campaign operations Familiarity with Google Analytics (GA4) and data visualization tools such as Google Data Studio is a plus Exposure to paid digital ad platforms (Google Ads, Meta Ads, etc.) preferred; strong willingness to learn is essential Detail-oriented and highly organized, with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines Strong written and verbal communication skills A strong desire to learn, grow, and excel; curious, resourceful, and driven by data Team-oriented, with an interest in marketing operations, process, and automation Salary Grade 7: $62,500 – $79,500 per year (depending on experience) Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.  Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Worcester/AV-Systems-Engineer-II_R0003684": {
